  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery shows multiple cyclones across the \nCONUS with one from the remnants of Hurricane Isaias over the \nsoutheastern US and the others towards the west coast - one digging \ninto the PNW and the other still as tropical storm Rachel that \ncontinues to approach the SoCal region. Over the central US, \nsouthwesterly flow dominates the region ahead of the troughing over \nthe western CONUS and continues to usher in warm air from the \nsouthwest and keep temperatures above-average across the Plains. \nExpect conditions this afternoon to reflect the synoptic features as \nefficient BL mixing helps to bring down warm 925 and 850 mb \ntemperatures. Highs topping out in the low 90s will be common. \nSimilar conditions persist into Sunday and again Monday. As the \nremnants of Isaias moves towards the east coast and mid-level \nridging amplifies between the western trough, temperatures across \ncentral and eastern Kansas should push into the low to mid 90s \nMonday - approaching records.\n\nA more active pattern should begin to approach from the west by \nTuesday as an increase in PVA over the central Rockies returns upper \nlevel forcing and moisture to the Plains. A few shortwaves embedded \nin the southwesterly flow can be expected Tuesday and Wednesday that \ncould help to push a surface boundary through the area and increase \nwidespread PoPs. There are hints that some cooling aloft Tuesday \nafternoon ahead of the surface boundary could create an environment \nfor strong to severe storms. That said, confidence in widespread \nstrong storms at this time is low due to uncertainty in how all the \nparameters will line up. By Wednesday and Thursday, long range \nensemble and deterministic guidance begins to deviate on upper level \ncyclone solutions as the Euro cuts off the western trough and begins \nto retrograde the upper low back towards the southwestern US while \nthe GFS phases the cutoff low with a digging trough over the \nnorthern Plains. Both solutions depict decent upper-level lift \nthrough Wednesday and Thursday and with the surface boundary likely \nstalling across eastern Kansas, widespread PoPs should persist \nacross eastern Kansas. Deviations between the long range guidance \nare fully notable by Friday and the weekend with the GFS bringing a \nvery strong long-wave trough through the Plains while the Euro keeps \nthe trough well north and east of the Plains.",
